# Passkeys backend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package. `auth:setup` wrote the passkey
registration API: a signed-in user adds a passkey from their account, lists
it, renames it and deletes it. Passkey login is not part of this layer yet,
so nothing here changes how anyone signs in. No boilerplate file needs to be
edited by hand. Delete this file once you've read it.

## What to do

1. Run `php artisan migrate` to create the `passkeys` table.
2. Set the relying party in `.env` (read by `config/passkeys.php`):

   ```dotenv
   PASSKEYS_RP_ID=localhost
   PASSKEYS_RP_NAME="${APP_NAME}"
   PASSKEYS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
   ```

   `PASSKEYS_RP_ID` is the frontend's host, or a registrable parent domain of
   it, without scheme or port. `PASSKEYS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` is a comma-separated
   list of full frontend origins (scheme, host, port). A ceremony from any
   other origin is rejected with `422`.
3. Use a cache store shared by every app server (the boilerplate's `database`
   store works). The registration challenge lives in the cache.

## Endpoints

All of them sit under `auth:sanctum` (the cookie session) in
`routes/passkeys.php`, which `routes/api.php` requires.

| Method | Path | Body | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `GET` | `/passkeys` | - | `200`, the user's passkeys: `id`, `name`, `created_at`, `last_used_at` |
| `POST` | `/passkeys/registration-options` | `{ password }` | `200`, the WebAuthn creation options for `navigator.credentials.create` |
| `POST` | `/passkeys` | `{ name, credential }` | `201`, the new passkey |
| `PATCH` | `/passkeys/{passkey}` | `{ name }` | `200`, the renamed passkey |
| `DELETE` | `/passkeys/{passkey}` | `{ password }` | `204` |

- Starting a registration and deleting a passkey both ask for the current
  password, like turning 2FA on and off: a passkey is a new way into the
  account, so a borrowed session must not be able to add or remove one. A
  wrong password is a `422` on the `password` field (`current_password`
  rule), not a `401`, so the frontend keeps the user signed in.
- `credential` is the `PublicKeyCredential` JSON the browser returns
  (`RegistrationResponseJSON`), sent as is, camelCase keys included.
- The challenge is bound to the user, lives
  `passkeys.challenge_ttl_seconds` (300 by default) and is spent by the first
  `POST /passkeys`, under a cache lock, so a replayed or expired one answers
  `410` (`passkey_challenge_expired`). A response that fails verification,
  malformed input included, answers `422` (`passkey_registration_failed`); a
  credential that is already stored answers `409`
  (`passkey_already_registered`).
- Another user's passkey answers `403` on rename and delete.
- Every route except the list uses `throttle:passkeys`: 6 requests a minute
  per user plus 30 a minute per IP. `lightit-auth-laravel`'s own service
  provider registers that limiter when
  `\Lightit\Authentication\Domain\PasskeyRateLimiter` exists, so the package
  must stay a runtime dependency (`composer require`, never `--dev`).

## How a passkey is stored

- `credential_id_hash` is the SHA-256 of the raw credential id, the fixed-length
  unique key for lookups. `credential_id` and `public_key` are stored
  base64url, never as raw bytes.
- The user handle sent to the authenticator is an HMAC of the user id keyed
  with `app.key`: stable per user, so a person's passkeys group together on
  their device, and not derivable from the id. Rotating `APP_KEY` changes it
  for passkeys registered afterwards.
- Every call into `web-auth/webauthn-lib` lives in
  `\Lightit\Authentication\Domain\Services\PasskeyCeremonyService`.
